= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For these <linear systems of divisors>, take a complex <K3 surface> $X$ and a nef big divisor $D$, so $D^2>0$. The exceptional terminology refers to the complete system $|D|$, rather than to an arbitrary subsystem.

A <monogonal linear system on a K3 surface> has the form
$$
D=mE+\Gamma,\qquad m\ge2,\quad
E^2=0,\quad E\Gamma=1,\quad\Gamma^2=-2,
\qquad |D|=\Gamma+|mE|,
$$
where $|E|$ is an <elliptic pencil on a K3 surface> and $\Gamma$ is its section. Its intersection with an elliptic fibre is one, and its fixed part is $\Gamma$. For a concrete existence example, take the smooth <Fermat quartic surface> $X\subseteq\mathbb P^3$ and two skew lines on it. Such lines can be written as
$$
\Gamma_0:\ x_0=\zeta x_1,\ x_2=\eta x_3,\qquad
\Gamma:\ x_0=\zeta' x_1,\ x_2=\eta' x_3,
$$
with $\zeta^4=(\zeta')^4=\eta^4=(\eta')^4=-1$, $\zeta\ne\zeta'$ and $\eta\ne\eta'$. The equations show that they are disjoint, and the four partial derivatives show that the quartic is smooth. The <adjunction formula> gives $K_X=0$, and the hypersurface <exact sequence of sheaves> $0\to\mathcal O_{\mathbb P^3}(-4)\to\mathcal O_{\mathbb P^3}\to\mathcal O_X\to0$, together with the intermediate <cohomology> vanishing of these twists, gives $H^1(X,\mathcal O_X)=0$. Thus it is a <K3 surface>, and each line has square $-2$. If $H$ is the hyperplane class, the planes through $\Gamma_0$ cut out $\Gamma_0$ plus a residual cubic pencil $|E|$, $E=H-\Gamma_0$. This pencil has no base point off $\Gamma_0$. On $\Gamma_0$, its two local residual equations are the two normal derivatives of the quartic equation; they cannot vanish simultaneously because $X$ is smooth. Thus it is <basepoint-free>, with
$$
E^2=4-2-2=0,\qquad E\Gamma=1.
$$
The degree-one map of $\Gamma$ to the pencil base makes it a section and forces connected fibres. <Bertini smoothness theorem> and <adjunction formula> then make a general fibre a smooth genus-one curve.

Now choose $D=mE+\Gamma$. For every irreducible curve other than $\Gamma$, both $E$ and the effective curve $\Gamma$ have nonnegative intersection with it. Also $D\Gamma=m-2\ge0$, so $D$ is nef, and $D^2=2m-2>0$. On a general elliptic fibre, the restriction of $\mathcal O_X(D)$ is the degree-one bundle of the section point. Its unique section vanishes at that point. Every <global section> of $\mathcal O_X(D)$ therefore vanishes along a dense subset of $\Gamma$, hence along $\Gamma$ itself. Removing this fixed divisor gives exactly $|mE|$. This verifies the monogonal example directly; its general divisor is a section plus $m$ fibres.

A <hyperelliptic linear system on a K3 surface> is a <basepoint-free> nef big system whose morphism is generically two-to-one. Equivalently its general smooth curve is <hyperelliptic>. Let $\pi:X\to\mathbb P^2$ be a double cover branched over a smooth sextic, and put $D=\pi^*\mathcal O(1)$. The double-cover construction gives
$$
\pi_*\mathcal O_X=\mathcal O\oplus\mathcal O(-3),\qquad
K_X=\pi^*(K_{\mathbb P^2}+3H)=0,\qquad
H^1(X,\mathcal O_X)=0.
$$
Thus $X$ is a <K3 surface>. Further,
$$
D^2=2,\qquad
H^0(X,D)=H^0(\mathbb P^2,\mathcal O(1)),
$$
since the second summand contributes $H^0(\mathcal O(-2))=0$. The map given by $|D|$ is precisely the double cover. A general member is the double cover of a line branched at six points, so <Riemann-Hurwitz formula> gives <genus> two. This is the hyperelliptic example.

A <trigonal linear system on a K3 surface> is a <basepoint-free> nonhyperelliptic nef big system whose general smooth curve has gonality three. For an example, take the same smooth quartic with a line $\Gamma_0$ and the hyperplane system $|H|$. Its general member is a <smooth plane quartic> of <genus> three; <adjunction formula> identifies its plane embedding with its <canonical map>, so it is not hyperelliptic. The elliptic pencil $E=H-\Gamma_0$ has
$$
H\cdot E=4-1=3.
$$
Its restriction to a general hyperplane curve is a <basepoint-free> degree-three pencil. The curve is neither rational nor hyperelliptic, so its gonality is exactly three. This verifies the trigonal example.

Thus the three examples exhibit respectively **a fixed section with elliptic fibres, a double-plane map, and an embedded quartic with trigonal hyperplane sections**. The last example also illustrates why low-genus trigonal sections should not be confused with a blanket numerical criterion for every higher-genus model.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Take $D$ nef and big on a complex <K3 surface>, and put $g=1+D^2/2$. The <K3 linear system dichotomies> are successive alternatives, so each later statement assumes that the exceptional cases in the earlier ones have been excluded.

The first dichotomy separates <basepoint-free> systems from <monogonal linear systems on a K3 surface>. In the exceptional case,
$$
D=mE+\Gamma,\quad m\ge2,\quad
E^2=0,\quad E\Gamma=1,\quad\Gamma^2=-2,
\qquad |D|=\Gamma+|mE|.
$$
Otherwise $|D|$ is <basepoint-free>. There are no isolated base points left after excluding the fixed-section case; this is the https://arxiv.org/pdf/alg-geom/9602006[first dichotomy].

For a <basepoint-free> system, the second dichotomy separates <hyperelliptic linear systems on a K3 surface> from birational maps to normal images. The degree-two cases are characterized by
$$
D^2=2,\qquad\text{or}\qquad
E^2=0,\ E\text{ an elliptic pencil},\ DE=2,
\qquad\text{or}\qquad D=2B,\ B^2=2,\ |B|\text{ basepoint-free}.
$$
Their images are surfaces of minimal degree: the plane, a <rational normal scroll>, or the <Veronese surface>. In the birational case, the map is an embedding away from the $(-2)$-curves $\Gamma$ with $D\Gamma=0$, which it contracts to <Du Val singularities>. Thus “birational” does not assert an embedding of the original surface. These are the https://www.math.uni-bonn.de/people/huybrech/K3Global.pdf[degree-two and birational projective-model alternatives].

In the third dichotomy, the birational image is either defined scheme-theoretically by quadrics or has an exceptional cubic requirement. The exceptional threefold containing it is $\mathbb P^3$ for $g=3$, a <quadric hypersurface> in $\mathbb P^4$ for $g=4$, a rational normal threefold <scroll> in the higher-genus trigonal case, or the cone over the <Veronese surface> in the plane-quintic case. For $g\ge5$, trigonal sections come from an elliptic pencil with $DE=3$; the plane-quintic exception has
$$
g=6,\qquad D=2B+\Gamma,\qquad
B^2=2,\ B\Gamma=1,\ \Gamma^2=-2,\quad |B|\text{ basepoint-free}.
$$
All other birational cases have <homogeneous ideal> generated by quadrics. For $g\ge4$, the exceptional ideals are generated by quadrics and cubics. For $g=3$, the image is a quartic hypersurface in $\mathbb P^3$ and its ideal needs its degree-four defining equation; that case must not be included in the cubic-generation assertion. The geometric alternatives, including the low-genus exceptions, are the https://arxiv.org/pdf/alg-geom/9602006[plane-quintic exception to the third dichotomy]; the higher-genus trigonal conclusion is also stated in https://web.math.ucsb.edu/~drm/conferences/ITP99/morrison/cortona.pdf[the projective-model theorem].

The point of the final exception is that a plane quintic is not a <trigonal curve>. Its canonical model is the degree-two Veronese embedding of the plane curve, and its quadrics retain an ambient <Veronese surface>. Accordingly, one must include the cone-over-Veronese case instead of calling every failure of quadratic generation “trigonal.”

