= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $\Lambda=H^2(X,\mathbb Z)$ with the cup-product pairing $q$ of signature $(3,19)$. For a <K3 surface>, the <Hodge numbers> are $(h^{2,0},h^{1,1},h^{0,2})=(1,20,1)$. A nonzero holomorphic two-form $\omega$ spans $F^2$, and its wedge products give
$$
q(\omega,\omega)=0,\qquad q(\omega,\bar\omega)>0.
$$
The remaining filtration is forced by orthogonality:
$$
F^2=\mathbb C\omega,\qquad F^1=\omega^\perp,
\qquad H^{1,1}=(\mathbb C\omega\oplus\mathbb C\bar\omega)^\perp.
$$
Thus the usual <K3 period domain> and its quadric compactification are
$$
\check D_\Lambda=\{[\omega]\in\mathbb P(\Lambda_{\mathbb C}):
q(\omega,\omega)=0\},\qquad
D_\Lambda=\{[\omega]\in\check D_\Lambda:q(\omega,\bar\omega)>0\}.
$$
The quadric is smooth and has complex dimension $21-1=20$. Writing $\omega=x+iy$, its equations say that $x,y$ are orthogonal positive vectors of equal square. A period therefore determines an oriented positive two-plane in $\Lambda_{\mathbb R}$.

There is a polarization qualification: the full cup-product form is not a polarization in the strict sense of part (a). Its real $(1,1)$ space has signature $(1,19)$, so it cannot satisfy the negative-definiteness requirement there. Fixing an ample integral class $h$ of square $2d>0$ and requiring it to stay of type $(1,1)$ gives the <primitive cohomology> lattice
$$
\Lambda_h=h^\perp,\qquad \operatorname{signature}(\Lambda_h)=(2,19).
$$
Its genuine polarized <Griffiths domain> is
$$
\boxed{D_h=\{[\omega]\in\mathbb P((\Lambda_h)_{\mathbb C}):
q(\omega,\omega)=0,\ q(\omega,\bar\omega)>0\}.}
$$
Its compact dual is the corresponding smooth quadric of complex dimension nineteen, and $D_h$ has two components, distinguished by the orientation of the positive two-plane. For ample-polarized geometric K3 moduli one also removes the loci $(\omega,\delta)=0$ with $\delta\in\Lambda_h$, $\delta^2=-2$, since an ample class cannot be orthogonal to an effective rational curve. Those exclusions concern the ample moduli locus; they are not part of the definition of the polarized Hodge domain.

If one wants a strict polarization on the full $H^2$ of a fixed projective surface, reverse the sign on its rational Kähler line and retain $q$ on the orthogonal complement. For example the integral form
$$
Q_h(u,v)=q(h,h)q(u,v)-2q(u,h)q(v,h)
$$
has the required positivity and signature $(2,20)$. It is a different form from the <cup product>. The twenty-dimensional full period quadric and the nineteen-dimensional primitive polarized domain should therefore be distinguished. The positivity and signature calculations above explain why these two domains describe different data.

= 5
{parent=Paper 21}
{scope}

= a
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A pure integral <variation of Hodge structure> of weight $w$ on a complex manifold $S$ consists of a <local system> $\mathbb H_{\mathbb Z}$ of finite-rank free abelian groups and a decreasing filtration by holomorphic subbundles
$$
F^p\mathcal H\subseteq\mathcal H
=\mathbb H_{\mathbb Z}\otimes_{\mathbb Z}\mathcal O_S.
$$
Each fibre, with this filtration and the conjugation from its integral lattice, must be a <Hodge structure> of weight $w$ with locally constant <Hodge numbers>. The <local system> gives a flat connection $\nabla$: in a locally constant frame it differentiates the coefficient functions. The filtration must satisfy <Griffiths transversality>,
$$
\boxed{\nabla(F^p\mathcal H)\subseteq
F^{p-1}\mathcal H\otimes\Omega_S^1.}
$$
A polarized variation additionally has a locally constant integral <bilinear form> $Q$ which polarizes every fibre. Flatness means $\nabla Q=0$. A collection of fibrewise Hodge decompositions without a holomorphic filtration or the transversality condition is not yet a <variation of Hodge structure>.

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Here a smooth family is understood as a <smooth proper family of compact complex manifolds>: $f:\mathcal X\to S$ is a proper holomorphic submersion, with compact Kähler fibres. Properness and compactness are essential for the usual statement. Indeed, the projection $(\mathbb C_t\times\mathbb C_z)\setminus\{(0,0)\}\to\mathbb C_t$ is a holomorphic submersion with Kähler fibres, but its fibres are $\mathbb C$ for $t\ne0$ and $\mathbb C^*$ for $t=0$. Their first <Betti numbers> are zero and one, so their <cohomology> cannot give a fixed-rank variation. This supplies a counterexample if “smooth family” is read without the usual proper compact-fibre hypothesis. The <Ehresmann fibration theorem> gives local differentiable trivializations, so
$$
\mathbb H_{\mathbb Z}=R^kf_*\mathbb Z/\text{torsion}
$$
is a <local system> with fibre $H^k(X_s,\mathbb Z)/\text{torsion}$. Its associated flat bundle has the <Gauss–Manin connection>, given by differentiating <cohomology> classes in topologically constant frames.

The <Kähler Hodge decomposition> gives
$$
H^k(X_s,\mathbb C)=\bigoplus_{p+q=k}H^{p,q}(X_s).
$$
The <Betti numbers> are locally constant by differentiable triviality. The dimensions of $H^q(X_s,\Omega^p_{X_s})$ are upper semicontinuous, and their sums for fixed $p+q=k$ equal the fixed <Betti number>. Near any fixed fibre, every summand can only decrease, while their sum cannot change; hence none decreases. Thus the <Hodge numbers> are locally constant. The <relative de Rham complex> and the <Grauert base change theorem> then make
$$
F^p_s=\bigoplus_{r\ge p}H^{r,k-r}(X_s)
$$
the fibres of holomorphic subbundles $F^p\mathcal H$. More intrinsically these are the images of the <hypercohomology> of the truncated <relative de Rham complex> $\Omega_{\mathcal X/S}^{\ge p}$ in the full relative de Rham <cohomology>. Kähler degeneration of the spectral sequence ensures that these images have the required ranks.

For the transversality proof, let $A^2$ denote the ideal in the absolute holomorphic de Rham complex generated by two pulled-back base one-forms. Keeping zero or one base differential gives a <short exact sequence of cochain complexes>, with the usual shifted differential in the left-hand complex
$$
0\longrightarrow f^*\Omega_S^1\otimes
\Omega_{\mathcal X/S}^{\bullet-1}
\longrightarrow
\Omega_{\mathcal X}^{\bullet}/A^2
\longrightarrow
\Omega_{\mathcal X/S}^{\bullet}
\longrightarrow0.
$$
The connecting morphism in degree $k$ is the <Gauss–Manin connection>
$$
\nabla:\mathcal H\longrightarrow\mathcal H\otimes\Omega_S^1.
$$
To compute it, lift a relative de Rham cocycle to the middle complex, apply the absolute differential, and retain the terms with one base differential. A cocycle in filtration degree $p$ can be represented using absolute forms of degree at least $p$. In a term containing one base differential, removing that factor leaves a relative form of degree at least $p-1$. The connecting construction therefore lands in $F^{p-1}$:
$$
\boxed{\nabla F^p\subseteq F^{p-1}\otimes\Omega_S^1.}
$$
This is the filtration shift behind <Griffiths transversality>. It explains the inequality rather than merely naming a theorem. The unfiltered construction agrees with topological parallel transport by the de Rham comparison, and its square is zero, as required for the flat connection.

Consequently the fibre <cohomology> gives a pure <variation of Hodge structure>. To claim a polarized integral variation one needs the additional compatible flat polarization. In a smooth projective family, a relative ample <line bundle> supplies a flat integral <Kähler class>; its primitive pieces are polarized by the <Hodge–Riemann bilinear relations>, and the full <cohomology> is polarized using the appropriate primitive-piece signs. For a general compact Kähler family, the unpolarized variation above is automatic, but a flat integral polarization is not supplied merely by knowing that each fibre is Kähler. This distinction is particularly relevant to unrestricted K3 families.

= c
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For a complex <K3 surface>, $L=H^2(X,\mathbb Z)$ is torsion-free and has its integral <cup product> pairing
$$
q(x,y)=\langle x\smile y,[X]\rangle.
$$
The <K3 intersection lattice> is
$$
\boxed{L\cong U^{\oplus3}\oplus E_8(-1)^{\oplus2},}
$$
the even unimodular <integral quadratic lattice> of signature $(3,19)$. Here $U$ is the <hyperbolic plane (quadratic form)> with Gram matrix
$$
\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix},
$$
and $E_8(-1)$ is the negative of the positive-definite <E8 lattice>. Thus the rank is $3\cdot2+2\cdot8=22$ and the pairing has determinant of absolute value one.

These invariants can be checked from the topology and <Hodge theory>. The trivial <canonical bundle> and $H^1(X,\mathcal O_X)=0$ give $\chi(\mathcal O_X)=2$. The <Noether formula> gives $c_2(X)=24$, which is the topological <Euler characteristic>. Since $X$ is simply connected, $b_1=b_3=0$, so $24=2+b_2$ and $b_2=22$. Integral <Poincare duality> gives unimodularity; the universal coefficient sequence and $H_1(X,\mathbb Z)=0$ give torsion-freeness of $H^2(X,\mathbb Z)$.

Its second <Stiefel–Whitney class> is the reduction modulo two of $c_1(X)$, hence zero. The characteristic-class identity
$$
\langle x^2,[X]\rangle
\equiv\langle x\smile w_2(X),[X]\rangle\pmod2
$$
makes the lattice even. Finally the real and imaginary parts of a holomorphic two-form give two positive directions, a <Kähler class> gives a third, and the primitive real $(1,1)$ classes are negative definite by the <Hodge–Riemann bilinear relations>. Therefore the signature is $(3,19)$. The classification of indefinite even unimodular lattices then identifies the displayed lattice uniquely. In particular the <Picard group> is only the integral $(1,1)$ part; it should not be confused with this rank-twenty-two topological lattice.

= d
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

One useful version is the effective <global Torelli theorem for K3 surfaces>. Let $X,Y$ be complex <K3 surfaces> and let
$$
\phi:H^2(Y,\mathbb Z)\xrightarrow{\sim}H^2(X,\mathbb Z)
$$
preserve the integral cup-product pairing and the Hodge decomposition. Suppose that $\phi$ carries some <Kähler class> of $Y$ to a <Kähler class> of $X$. Then
$$
\boxed{\text{there is a unique isomorphism }f:X\xrightarrow{\sim}Y
\text{ such that }f^*=\phi.}
$$
Conversely every <isomorphism> has these properties. In particular, for projective <K3 surfaces> an integral Hodge isometry carrying an ample polarization to an ample polarization is induced by a unique <isomorphism> of the polarized surfaces. This is the https://www.math.uni-bonn.de/people/huybrech/K3Global.pdf[effective global Torelli statement].

The Kähler-cone condition is necessary if the prescribed isometry itself is to be induced by an <isomorphism>. If $\delta$ is the class of an effective smooth rational $(-2)$-curve, the reflection
$$
r_\delta(x)=x+(x,\delta)\delta
$$
is integral, preserves the pairing, and preserves the Hodge decomposition because $\delta$ has type $(1,1)$. It sends $\delta$ to $-\delta$. An <automorphism> preserves effectivity, whereas $-\delta$ is not effective: it has negative intersection with an ample class. Thus this Hodge isometry is not an <automorphism> pullback. Requiring an isometry to respect the Kähler chamber removes precisely this kind of obstruction.
